//
// The runnable half of `1453-a-nullable-collection-is-refused-by-name-in-the-collection-builtins`,
// which stops at its expected errors and so can assert nothing.  This half is where the claims
// are checked, and it is the half that keeps that refusal honest.
//
// Two things are proven here and nowhere else:
//
//   * the DENSE spelling of every refused cell is correct AND silent, which is what says the
//     refusal is about the `?` and not about the lambda or the builtin;
//   * BOTH cures the message advertises — `?` and `?? []` — compile and answer the RIGHT
//     VALUES.  A message whose cure is itself diagnosed advertises a dead end, and one whose
//     cure compiles but answers wrongly is worse.
//
// The absent receiver is the cell that makes "an empty result" a value rather than a phrase:
// `map(absent?, …)` is zero elements, not one and not a fault.
//
// @falsified-at: none — INERT by construction, and measured rather than assumed.
//   `scripts/falsify.sh … c965234d` (the parent of this file's own fix) reports
//   `0|0|none|none|0|-` on both trees and both backends: NOT falsified.
//   That is correct here and is the point of the file. loft#1453's fix changed a
//   DIAGNOSTIC; the cures it advertises — `?` and `?? []` — already compiled and already
//   answered these values before it, so nothing this file asserts could have been red on
//   the control. Its job is the opposite direction: to fail on the day one of the
//   advertised cures stops working, which would leave the refusal in `1453-…` pointing at
//   a dead end. A companion to a refusal file, not a regression guard for the fix.

struct It1453b { k: integer, v: integer }

fn main() {
  present: vector<It1453b>? = [It1453b { k: 1, v: 7 }, It1453b { k: 2, v: 9 }];
  absent: vector<It1453b>? = null;
  dense: vector<It1453b> = [It1453b { k: 1, v: 7 }, It1453b { k: 2, v: 9 }];

  // ---- control: the DENSE spelling of each refused cell, correct and silent ----
  d_map = map(dense, |e| { e.v });
  assert(len(d_map) == 2, "the dense receiver maps every element: {len(d_map)}");
  assert(d_map[0] == 7, "and keeps its order: {d_map[0]}");
  d_filter = filter(dense, |e| { e.v > 5 });
  assert(len(d_filter) == 2, "the dense receiver filters: {len(d_filter)}");
  assert(any(dense, |e| { e.v > 8 }), "and answers a predicate");

  // ---- cure 1, `?`, on a PRESENT receiver ----
  cure_default = map(present?, |e| { e.v });
  assert(len(cure_default) == 2, "`?` discharges and maps every element: {len(cure_default)}");
  assert(cure_default[1] == 9, "with the right values: {cure_default[1]}");
  assert(len(filter(present?, |e| { e.v > 8 })) == 1, "and it works for filter too");

  // ---- cure 2, `?? []`, answering the same ----
  cure_coalesce = map(present ?? [], |e| { e.v });
  assert(len(cure_coalesce) == 2, "`?? []` maps the same elements: {len(cure_coalesce)}");
  assert(cure_coalesce[0] == 7, "with the same values: {cure_coalesce[0]}");

  // ---- the cure on an ACTUALLY-absent receiver: "an empty result" is a value ----
  cure_absent = map(absent?, |e| { e.v });
  assert(len(cure_absent) == 0, "an absent receiver discharged gives an EMPTY result: {len(cure_absent)}");
  assert(len(map(absent ?? [], |e| { e.v })) == 0, "and `?? []` agrees");
  assert(!any(absent?, |e| { e.v > 0 }), "a predicate over an absent one answers for an empty one");
}
